3.4.10
# The development Ruby pin. Line 1 is the version and nothing else, and every
# other line is a `#` comment — because the common readers of this file each
# take the FIRST WORD or the FIRST LINE (rbenv's `rbenv-version-file-read`
# reads the first word; mise reads the first line), and a comment placed
# above the version would be what they read. Line 1 first, comments after.
#
# Why this version, and what it replaced:
#
# This file pinned 3.2.3, which the ruby-lang.org advisory for CVE-2024-27282
# lists as affected ("Ruby 3.2.3 or lower"). 3.4.10 is not affected: it is a
# later release in a series the advisory never lists, and it is above the
# advisory's own fixed release for every series the advisory names.
#
# Advisory (authoritative, ruby-lang.org):
#   https://www.ruby-lang.org/en/news/2024/04/23/arbitrary-memory-address-read-regexp-cve-2024-27282/
#   "CVE-2024-27282: Arbitrary memory address read vulnerability with Regex
#    search", published 2024-04-23.
#
# Affected versions, verbatim from that advisory:
#   Ruby 3.0.6 or lower
#   Ruby 3.1.4 or lower
#   Ruby 3.2.3 or lower
#   Ruby 3.3.0
#
# Fixed versions, verbatim from that advisory:
#   Ruby 3.0.7, Ruby 3.1.5, Ruby 3.2.4, Ruby 3.3.1
#
# 3.4.10 satisfies that record on its own terms: it is greater than every
# affected version above and greater than every fixed version above, so it is
# not among the releases the advisory marks affected. It is also a real
# upstream release, not a local build — ruby-lang.org published it on
# 2026-06-30 (https://www.ruby-lang.org/en/news/2026/06/30/ruby-3-4-10-released/).
#
# Two constraints this pin has to satisfy at the same time:
#
#   * It must be >= 3.2, the gemspec's `required_ruby_version`
#     (apps/magik/magik.gemspec). 3.4.10 is above that floor. The floor is
#     NOT changed by this pin: the gem still supports CRuby >= 3.2, and CI
#     still proves that floor on its own 3.2 / 3.3 / 3.4 matrix.
#   * It must name an UPSTREAM RELEASE that a Ruby manager on the reader's
#     box can install and resolve — the same release `mise.toml` names and
#     the same release the two bin/setup fix lines install. It need NOT be
#     installed already: `mise install ruby@3.4.10 && mise exec ruby@3.4.10
#     -- bin/setup` is the shipped remedy, and it installs what is not there
#     yet. 3.4.10 satisfies that — it is an upstream release a manager
#     installs and then resolves.
#
#     The advisory's minimum patched release, 3.2.4, is not named here for a
#     compatibility reason, not a security one: this box's mise has no
#     core:ruby@3.2.x, so a 3.2.x here means a from-source build on first
#     `ruby` — the stall that made bin/setup die with
#     MAGIK_DEV_RUBY_TOO_OLD before. 3.4.10 needs no such build. See
#     mise.toml.
#
# A box that does not run mise at all resolves the pin through whichever
# manager it does run — rbenv on the reference dev container
# (.devcontainer/Dockerfile ships CRuby 3.2 and rbenv, no mise). The pin is
# the single statement of which release this checkout develops against; the
# fix lines are what put that release in front of the reader's box.
#
# This pin is dev-box only. CI installs its own engine matrix and never reads
# this file, and TruffleRuby remains the production runtime target.
